o ask  the question  what's going on what's in the smoke then  they start telling us  so2 co2 what's that mean  those are the things those are the  poisons and the pollutants  that are floating around in the air that  are killing your grass  because not only are you killing the  trees you're also killing people  tens of thousands of black people fled  segregation in the south  to live near here drawn to the booming  auto industry in detroit  teresa landrum said it was a utopia  but not anymore african americans  have been put into or forced to live in  areas that are polluted so environmental  racism has a lot to  do with where we live and where these  factories  and these industries are located  marathon the company which runs this  huge oil refinery  has offered to buy up homes to create a  buffer zone  it says it's reduced emissions by 80  over 20 years and now produces less than  three percent in a two mile radius  local activists though insist it all  adds up  [Music]  and rhonda anderson another cancer  survivor  wants radical change the demand would be  to shut down  all the fossil fuel places all of them  they don't need to be operating the  first thing that's going to do  is help those new babies you know  because right now  our babies are doomed  before they reach kindergarten  this isn't about one firm or one place  there's toxic air lead poisoning and  water shutoffs  across america big business and  government are under more pressure than  ever before  they've got a long way to go cordelia  lynch  sky news and cordelia also spoke to  renowned environmental campaigner erin  brockovich about michigan's most  polluted  zip code and you can see that interview  here on the daily climate show  tomorrow let's take a look now at some  of the day's other climate news  and it might be impossible to keep  global temperature rises  under 2 degrees above pre-industrial  levels  if the cop 26 summit that's due to be  held in glasgow  fails that's the conclusion from a group  of mps who say the climate summit needs  to be focused on the targets for success  well before it actually happens in  november tom  tugan hart is the chair of the foreign  affairs committee who released the  report  some of the things that we need to think  about is whether or not  you know how we count the carbon output  of an item whether we count it  tied to the uk as it was manufactured  here even though it was manufactured in  somewhere where  carbon emissions are more liberally  interpreted  [Applause]  the cost of financing renewable energy  projects is four times less than  investing in coal  a new study from the university of  oxford has found that the risk of  lending for new fossil fuel initiatives  has increased as the world moves to  cleaner energy sources  the most sustainable olympics was more  than 18 years ago in 2002.  the salt lake city games scored highest  in an analysis published today in nature  magazine  and has declined ever since researchers  measured how well each city performed in  economic  social and ecological sustainability the  report recommends  rotating between existing venues and  host cities  as well as reducing the number of  in-person spectators  [Applause]  one of the most common industrial  fishing methods could be releasing  millions of tons of carbon stored in the  seabed  the marine conservation society has  warned that bottom trawling where a  weighted net is pulled along the sea  floor to catch fish can damage the uk's  most vulnerable  sea habitats polling commissioned by  greenpeace  says that two-thirds of people now back  a ban on bottom trawling in marine  protected areas  and the world's most powerful tidal  turbine is beginning its journey to be  installed off the coast of scotland  the orbital o2 turbine will have the  capacity to generate electricity for  2 000 homes a year once it arrives at  the european marine energy center  in the waters of orkney now let's take  you over to our data dashboard now  and show you this number here now this  shows how much  human activity has warmed our planet  since the industrial revolution and it's  ticking up every single second  the aim agreed by 196 parties in 2015  as part of the paris agreement is to  keep that under two degrees preferably  to 1.5 degrees of warming and a crucial  report from the united nations has been  released today which found that in 2020  the global average temperature was about  1.2 degrees celsius  above pre-industrial levels so the state  of the global climate report  sets out how climate change indicators  and impacts  worsened around the world last year and  it found that  2020 was one of the three warmest years  on record  that's despite a cooling la nina event  now although lockdowns temporarily  reduce greenhouse gas emissions  overall atmospheric concentrations of  gases such as carbon dioxide and methane  continue to rise more than four-fifths  of seas  experienced at least one marine heat  wave last year  and there were record high temperatures  in the arctic circle in siberia and  arctic  sea ice shrank to its second lowest  summer minimum  on record well let's bring in our  climate change correspondent lisa  holland now and lisa not all of these  findings are new so what's the  significance of this  significance of this report being  released now well anna i think the  significance is in its timing in that it  comes just a couple of days before this  crucial virtual summit which is being  hosted  by the united states when america will  implore  other countries to up their ambitions to  reduce greenhouse gases  now this conference which will come up  towards the end of the week is seen as  really pivotal  and this report helps underline just how  critical  the situation is not just with some of  the data which we already knew about  just how  warm the planet has got but with some of  the really stark language which is being  used  by the world meteorological organization  and the united 